  - [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m — removes it (needed for logout, and for tests to reset state)

  [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m is deliberately not a separate function; [38;5;153mgetUser()?.userId[39m covers it and keeps the surface at three. If
  future forms only ever want the id, that's a one-line addition then.

  [1mStored shape.[0m This is the part that's expensive to change later, because whatever we write today may already be
  sitting in a user's [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m when a future version reads it. Two candidates:

  - [1mA bare string[0m under key [38;5;153muserId[39m — [38;5;153msessionStorage.setItem("userId", userId)[39m. Simplest possible read. But once a real
    server-issued id arrives, the username is no longer recoverable from storage, so any form that wants to greet the
    user by name has to re-derive it from somewhere. Widening a bare string to an object later is a breaking read for
    anything already written.
  - [1mA JSON record[0m under key [38;5;153msession.user[39m — [38;5;153m{"userId": "...", "username": "..."}[39m. Today both fields hold the same value,
    which looks redundant; the point is that they're the two things that [3mdiverge[0m the moment [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m becomes a real
    call and returns a server id. Consumers key off [38;5;153muserId[39m and display [38;5;153musername[39m, and neither has to change at the swap.
    Costs a [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m and a [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m for malformed data.

  I'm deliberately [1mnot[0m including a [38;5;153mloggedInAt[39m timestamp or a schema version field — neither is needed by anything you've
  described, and both are easy to add later.

  [1mI recommend the JSON record.[0m The whole reason this became architectural is that other forms will read this, and the
  record is what lets the id and the display name part ways later without a migration.

